WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.859 --> 00:00:05.710
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér

00:00:05.710 --> 00:00:09.459
Reinforcement learning is a technique that
can learn how to play computer games, or any

00:00:09.459 --> 00:00:14.709
kind of activity that requires a sequence
of actions. We're not interested in figuring

00:00:14.709 --> 00:00:20.120
out what we see on an image, because the answer
is one thing. We're always interested in a

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:22.210
sequence of actions.

00:00:22.210 --> 00:00:26.800
The input for reinforcement learning is a
state that describes where we are and how

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:32.160
the world looks around us, and the algorithm
outputs the optimal next action to take.

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:37.550
In this case, we would like a digital dog
to run, and leap over and onto obstacles by

00:00:37.550 --> 00:00:42.510
choosing the optimal next action. It is quite
difficult as there are a lot of body parts

00:00:42.510 --> 00:00:47.880
to control in harmony: the algorithm has to
be able to decide how to control leg forces,

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:53.020
spine curvature, angles for the shoulder,
elbow, hip, and knees.

00:00:53.020 --> 00:00:57.940
And what is really amazing is that if it has
learned everything properly, it will come

00:00:57.940 --> 00:01:03.160
up with exactly the same movements as we'd
expect animals to do in real life!

00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:07.850
So this is how reinforcement learning works:
If you do well, you get a reward, and if you

00:01:07.850 --> 00:01:12.710
don't, you get some kind of punishment. These
rewards and punishments are usually encoded

00:01:12.710 --> 00:01:15.600
in a score, which is a number that indicates
how well you're doing. If your score is increasing,

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:20.170
you know you've done something right and you
try to self-reflect and analyze the last few

00:01:20.170 --> 00:01:24.920
actions to find out which of them were responsible
for this positive change.

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:29.869
The score would be, for instance, how far
the dog could run on the map without falling,

00:01:29.869 --> 00:01:34.060
and at the same time, and it also makes sense
to minimize the amount of effort to make it

00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:35.119
happen.

00:01:35.119 --> 00:01:40.240
So, reinforcement learning in a nutshell.
It is very similar to how a real-world animal,

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:44.479
or even a human would learn - if you're not
doing well, try something new, and if you're

00:01:44.479 --> 00:01:49.549
succeeding, remember what you did that led
to your success and keep doing that.

00:01:49.549 --> 00:01:54.659
In this technique, dogs were used to demonstrate
a concept, but it's worth noting that it also

00:01:54.659 --> 00:01:56.920
works with bipeds.

00:01:56.920 --> 00:02:01.040
Reinforcement learning is typically used in
many control situations that are extremely

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:06.220
difficult to solve otherwise, like controlling
a quadrocopter properly. It's quite delightful

00:02:06.220 --> 00:02:11.470
to see such a cool work, especially given
that there are not so many uses of reinforcement

00:02:11.470 --> 00:02:17.040
learning in computer graphics yet. I wonder
why that is? Is it that not so many graphical

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:22.849
tasks require a sequence of actions? Or maybe
we just need to shift our mindset and get

00:02:22.849 --> 00:02:27.950
used to the idea of formalizing problems in
a different way so we can use such powerful

00:02:27.950 --> 00:02:31.450
techniques to solve them. It is definitely
worth the effort.

00:02:31.450 --> 00:02:35.610
Thanks for watching, and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

